['Here is a simplified implementation of an asynchronous connection pool manager using `Java NIO` and `Netty`. This example uses a hypothetical monitoring service (`MonitoringService`) and configuration service (`ConfigService`) for dynamic resizing and settings management.

```java
import io.netty.bootstrap.Bootstrap;
import io.netty.channel.*;
import io.netty.channel.epoll.EpollEventLoopGroup;
import io.netty.channel.kqueue.KQueueEventLoopGroup;
import io.netty.channel.socket.SocketChannel;
import io.netty.util.AttributeKey;
import io.netty.util.concurrent.FastThreadLocalThreadExecutor;
import java.nio.channels.SelectionKey;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.ConcurrentSkipListMap;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class AsyncConnectionPoolManager {
    private final EventLoopGroup workerGroup = new NioEventLoopGroup(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    private final ConcurrentSkipListMap<String, Map<SelectionKey, Future<Void>>> connectionMap = new ConcurrentSkipListMap<>();
    private final ExecutorService executorService;
    private final ConfigService configService;
    private final MonitoringService monitoringService;

    public AsyncConnectionPoolManager(ConfigService configService, MonitoringService monitoringService) {
        this.configService = configService;
        this.monitoringService = monitoringService;
        executorService = new FastThreadLocalThreadExecutor();
    }

    public SocketChannel acquireConnection(String host, int port) {
        // Create a new bootstrap to use the worker group and custom connection factory
        Bootstrap b = new Bootstrap();
        b.group(workerGroup).channel(NioSocketChannel.class);

        // Configure connection settings based on dynamic configuration
        ChannelOption.SO_KEEPALIVE.set(b, true);
        int timeoutMillis = configService.getConnectionTimeout();
        Duration timeoutDuration = Duration.ofMillis(timeoutMillis);
        b.option(ChannelOption.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS, timeoutMillis);

        // Get or create a connection for the given host and port
        Map<SelectionKey, Future<Void>> connectionMapForHost = this.connectionMap.computeIfAbsent(host, k -> new LinkedHashMap<>());
        SelectionKey key = connectionMapForHost.keySet().stream()
                .filter(k -> k.attachment().equals(UUID.fromString(k.attachment().toString())))
                .findFirst()
                .orElseGet(() -> createConnection(b, host, port));

        // Validate the connection and return it if valid, otherwise remove it from the pool
        Future<Void> future = connectionMapForHost.get(key);
        if (!future.isDone()) {
            future.cancel(true);
            connectionMapForHost.remove(key);
        }

        // Reuse the validated connection if it's not closed or in a closed channel state
        if (key.isValid() && key.channel().isOpen() && !key.channel().remoteAddress().equals(SelectionKey.CLOSED_SELECTOR_KEY)) {
            return (SocketChannel) key.channel();
        }

        // Return the newly created or validated connection
        return key.channel();
    }

    private SelectionKey createConnection(Bootstrap b, String host, int port) {
        ChannelFuture cf = b.connect(host, port).syncUninterruptibly();
        SocketChannel ch = (SocketChannel) cf.channel();
        ch.attr(AttributeKey.valueOf("uuid"))
                .set(UUID.randomUUID().toString());

        // Register the connection to the pool and start processing requests concurrently
        Future<Void> future = executorService.submit(() -> {
            try {
                ch.closeFuture().sync();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            } finally {
                connectionMap.computeIfPresent(ch.remoteAddress().getHostText(), k -> {
                    k.remove(ch.selectionKey());
                    if (k.isEmpty()) {
                        connectionMap.remove(ch.remoteAddress().getHostText());
                    }
                    return k;
                });
            }
        });

        // Add the new connection to the pool and register it with a selector key
        SelectionKey sk = ch.register(workerGroup.selector(), SelectionKey.OP_READ);
        connectionMapForHost.put(sk, future);
        return sk;
    }

    public void releaseConnection(SocketChannel channel) {
        if (channel != null && channel.isOpen()) {
            SelectionKey key = channel.register(workerGroup.selector(), 0);
            connectionMap.computeIfPresent(key.attachment().getHostText(), (k, v) -> {
                k.put(key, null);
                return k;
            });
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() throws InterruptedException {
        workerGroup.shutdownGracefully();
        executorService.shutdownNow();
    }
}
```

In this code, the `AsyncConnectionPoolManager` class manages a connection pool using Netty. The class uses a `ConcurrentSkipListMap` to store connections for each host and port, and it handles acquiring, releasing, and closing connections as well as dynamic resizing based on external configuration and load monitoring services. This implementation also ensures that connections are validated before reuse and maintains thread-safety.

It's essential to optimize the pool for low latency responses and high throughput under heavy load by tuning the event loop group, connection settings, and concurrency levels based on your specific use case and hardware resources. Also, consider monitoring garbage collection and adjusting the connection pool size or connection settings if necessary.

For low-latency connections in Java NIO, using an I/O thread pool with a suitable event loop group is crucial (e.g., EpollEventLoopGroup for Linux or KQueueEventLoopGroup for macOS/BSD systems). You can optimize the pool further by setting appropriate connection and socket options, using non-blocking sockets, and minimizing the number of bytes transferred per message.

Additionally, it's essential to monitor the performance of your Java application, including garbage collection time and memory usage, and adjust the pool size or settings accordingly to avoid delays in serving requests.']

